From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 06:25:13 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] udmabuf: Take the page that holds the end of a memfd

UDMABUF_CREATE takes whole pages, and memfd_pin_folios() takes nothing
at or past the end of the file. A range that ends in the file's last,
partial page therefore fails with -EINVAL although that page is whole in
the page cache and holds only zeroes past the end. With pages larger
than 4 KiB this is the common case: a Wayland client's 1536x1972 XRGB
buffer of 12115968 bytes ends inside a 16 KiB page, so a compositor
cannot import it and copies it instead.

Pin up to the end of the file when the range ends in its last page.
Shrinking the file is sealed against, so the page stays.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/dma-buf/udmabuf.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma-buf/udmabuf.c b/drivers/dma-buf/udmabuf.c
index 4a9ab58..9a8675f 100644
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/udmabuf.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/udmabuf.c
@@ -309,10 +309,19 @@ static long udmabuf_pin_folios(struct udmabuf *ubuf, struct file *memfd,
 	u32 cur_folio, cur_pgcnt;
 	pgoff_t pgoff, pgcnt;
 	long nr_folios;
-	loff_t end;
+	loff_t end, isize;
 
 	pgcnt = size >> PAGE_SHIFT;
 	end = start + (pgcnt << PAGE_SHIFT) - 1;
+	/*
+	 * The page that holds the end of the file is whole in the page cache,
+	 * past the end only zeroes: take it when the range ends there. A
+	 * client's buffer often ends inside a page on kernels with pages larger
+	 * than 4 KiB, and memfd_pin_folios() takes nothing past the end.
+	 */
+	isize = i_size_read(file_inode(memfd));
+	if (end >= isize && start < isize && start + size <= PAGE_ALIGN(isize))
+		end = isize - 1;
 	nr_folios = memfd_pin_folios(memfd, start, end, folios, pgcnt, &pgoff);
 	if (nr_folios <= 0)
 		return nr_folios ? nr_folios : -EINVAL;
